        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <p>OBJECTIVES:<p>
This experiment was flown by students participating in the 1985 Space Life Sciences
Training Program (SLSTP). The purpose of the experiment was: 1) to teach the students how
to use scientific method to answer scientific questions; 2) to teach them what is involved
in preparing an experiment to fly in the Space Shuttle Middeck; and 3) to have them become
acquainted with NASA Space Shuttle procedures. <br>
<br>
Scientifically, the objectives of this experiment were: 1) to verify the relationship
between soil moisture content and plant growth of Helianthus annuus seedlings; 2) to test
the effect of lower relative humidity on plant development and in particular, the growth
of roots above the soil; and 3) determine the effect on growth and development of corn
(Zea mays) seedlings in selected plant media in microgravity as compared to 1 gravity
control plants in the same culture system.<p>APPROACH:<p>
Fifty-seven dwarf sunflowers (Helianthus annuus cv. Teddy Bear) were planted in pots
containing sterile soil (Promix). Two different levels of soil moisture content were used.
Twenty-eight plants were planted in pots with a 70% soil moisture content and 29 in pots
having a 60% soil moisture content. Twenty-nine pots were fitted with an elongated top to
permit plants to grow in an enclosed moist environment. Plants were grown in twenty-eight
pots without the tops to allow the plants to grow in a drier (lower relative humidity)
environment. <br>
<br>
Twenty-five corn (Zea mays) were planted in pots containing different planting media.
Fourteen were planted in Promix (sterile soil), four in Groden (sterilized green wool),
and seven were wrapped in Whatmann filter paper moistened with distilled water. All of the
corn pots were fitted with elongated tops.<br>
<br>
All the pots were placed into the Plant Carry-on Container (PCOC) which was an aluminum
container with a battery-operated temperature recorder. The PCOC was loaded into the Space
Shuttle Middeck 12 hours before launch. The PCOC was totally passive and required no
electrical, thermal or telemetry interfaces, and no inflight manipulation or observation
by the crew. <br>
<br>
After landing at Dryden Flight Research Center (DFRC), initial measurements of the plants
were made. The pots were each weighed, photographed and described. The photographic data
of the plants was used to determine shoot and root length at the time of landing. The
plants were returned to the Kennedy Space Center (KSC) 20 hours after landing. <br>
<br>
At KSC, twenty-five plants were watered and placed in a 25 degreeC growth chamber under
continuous light conditions. The remaining pots from each treatment group were dried and
the moisture content was determined. <br>
<br>
The students were involved in all aspects of the experiment: (1) aseptically planting the
seeds, (2) loading the PCOC into the Space Shuttle , and (3) participating in the recovery
at DFRC.<p>RESULTS:<p>
Very little data analysis was conducted on the plant tissue returned from this experiment,
however, the following observations were made. In all treatment conditions growth and
germination was considered satisfactory. Generally, the plants in the pots without tops
had reduced growth and germination. Other observations indicated that soil moisture loss
occurred in pots without tops; there was littleif any loss in the pots with tops. The
plants growing in pots containing 70% soil moisture had fewer aerial roots than those
grown in 60% soil moisture. Plants grown in pots with tops and containing 60% soil
moisture produced the greatest number of aerial roots, which was consistent with
observations made during the STS-3 mission. <br>
<br>
All plants that germinated appeared healthy at the time of landing. Of the 25 seedlings
transferred to the growth chamber after landing, only five were able to recover and grow
to a flowering stage. The remaining 20 plants withered and died shortly after being placed
in the growth chamber. This result was significantly different from ground controls in
which 20 of the 25 plants grew to a flowering stage. <br>
</p>
